{
  "id": "physics-008",
  "title": "Why can't we hear sound in space?",
  "category": "physics",
  "subject_area": "sound waves",
  "difficulty_level": "introductory",
  "target_audience": [
    "general public",
    "primary school",
    "secondary school"
  ],
  "central_question": "Why is space silent, even when huge explosions happen in it?",
  "template": "principle",
  "principle_statement": "Sound is a vibration that has to pass through matter, so where there is almost no matter, as in space, sound cannot travel to our ears.",
  "domain_of_validity": "Sound in air, water, solids and near-empty space; thin gas and dust can carry sound waves that humans cannot hear.",
  "scientific_explanation": {
    "level_1": "Sound is a vibration that moves from one bit of matter to the next, like a wave passing along a row of people. When you talk, you make the air vibrate, and that vibration carries to a friend's ear. Space is mostly empty, so there is almost nothing to vibrate. With nothing to carry it, sound cannot travel, and space is silent.",
    "level_2": "Sound waves need a medium to move through, such as air, water or a solid like a table. In the empty vacuum of space there is no medium, so sound cannot travel. Space is not completely empty, though: large areas of gas and dust could carry sound waves. But the particles are so spread out that the waves have a very low frequency, which humans cannot hear.",
    "level_3": "Sound is a mechanical wave, a disturbance passed from particle to particle. For a wave, the speed equals the frequency times the wavelength, v = f L, and the wave needs a medium whose particles can pass the vibration on. Where matter is extremely thin, the sound waves that do exist have such low frequencies that humans cannot hear them. This is why, in space, no sound reaches our ears."
  },
  "supporting_evidence": [
    "On Earth, sound travels to your ears through vibrating air.",
    "Sound also travels through solids: it can go directly through a table to your ears."
  ],
